<p>
  The People section keeps your team in one place: who they are, what they do, who they report to,
  and when they're away.
</p>

<h2>Adding employees</h2>
<p>
  Admins click <strong>+ Employee</strong>
  under <.link navigate="/hr/employees">Employees</.link>. Fill in
  the person, their job (title, department, start date, contract) and who they <strong>report to</strong>.
  Each employee gets a number (<code>EMP-0001</code>) and also appears in your <.link navigate="/contacts">contacts</.link>, tagged <em>employee</em>.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Workspace login</strong>: link the employee to their Zippiker user so they can request leave
    and approve their team's requests. (Invite them first under <.link navigate="/settings/workspace">Settings → Workspace</.link>.)
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Pay &amp; personal details</strong>
    (salary, date of birth, ID, emergency contact) are visible
    only to admins and to the employee themselves.
  </li>
  <li>
    When someone leaves, open their page and click <strong>Mark as left</strong>. They move to
    <em>Former</em>
    with their history kept, and can be rehired.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Departments and the org chart</h2>
<p>
  Group people under <.link navigate="/hr/departments">Departments</.link>
  (each can have a head and sit
  inside another). The <.link navigate="/hr/org-chart">Org chart</.link>
  draws everyone from the top down,
  using <em>Reports to</em>. Zippiker stops you from creating loops where someone ends up managing
  their own manager.
</p>

<h2>Leave types</h2>
<p>
  Admins set them up under <.link navigate="/hr/leave-types">Settings → Leave types</.link>:
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Days per year</td><td>
        The allowance, in working days per calendar year. Empty means no limit.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Paid</td><td>Marks paid vs unpaid leave (for payroll later).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Needs approval</td><td>
        Off: requests are approved straight away (handy for sick leave).
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Leave policies</h2>
<p>
  A policy says how one leave type works for the people on it. Admins set them up under
  <.link navigate="/hr/leave-policies">Leave policies</.link>
  (<strong>+ Policy</strong>):
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Leave category</td>
      <td>
        <strong>Fixed yearly allowance</strong>
        (days per year, with optional carry-over of unused days), <strong>Accrual</strong>
        (days earned every week, month, quarter or year, up to a maximum balance),
        <strong>Manual</strong>
        (only what admins add and approved claims) or <strong>Unlimited</strong>.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Effective date</td>
      <td>When the policy starts. Accruals count from here (or from the hire date if later).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Review overdue threshold</td>
      <td>
        If the manager hasn't decided within this many days, the request is automatically
        <strong>approved</strong>
        or <strong>rejected</strong>
        (or left waiting), and marked as decided automatically.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Requires file upload</td>
      <td>Requests need a supporting document, e.g. a doctor's note.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Count weekends and holidays</td>
      <td>Every calendar day counts, instead of working days only.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Allow backdated requests</td>
      <td>Off: people can't request leave for past dates (admins still can).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Allow claims</td>
      <td>People can claim extra days, e.g. time off in lieu after working a weekend.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  Each leave type can have several policies. Click <strong>People</strong>
  on a policy to choose who's on it (or a whole department); everyone else follows the type's
  <strong>default</strong>
  policy. Without any policy, the leave type's own allowance applies.
</p>
<.callout>
  Accruals are credited automatically every night. Admins can also click
  <strong>Run accruals now</strong>
  after changing a policy. Missed nights catch up, and no period is ever credited twice.
</.callout>

<h2>Balances, adjustments and claims</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    Each employee's page shows their balance per leave type and which policy applies. Admins click
    <strong>Adjust balance</strong>
    to add days (or take them away with a negative number), e.g. an opening balance.
  </li>
  <li>
    On <.link navigate="/hr/leave-claims">Leave claims</.link>, people claim days on leave types that
    allow it. Their manager or an admin approves, and the days are added to their balance.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Holidays</h2>
<p>
  Add public holidays under <.link navigate="/hr/holidays">Holidays</.link>; tick
  <em>Same day every year</em>
  for fixed dates. Holidays and weekends aren't counted as leave days, unless the policy counts them.
</p>

<h2>Requesting time off</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    Go to <.link navigate="/hr/leave">Leave</.link> and click <strong>Request leave</strong>.
  </li>
  <li>
    Pick the type and dates. Zippiker counts the <strong>working days</strong>
    (Monday to Friday, minus public holidays) and shows your balance. Attach a supporting document
    if the policy asks for one.
  </li>
  <li>Your manager sees it under <strong>To approve</strong> and approves or rejects it.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Requests that overlap one you already have, or that go over your allowance, are refused with a clear
  message. You can cancel your own request at any time; days come back to your balance.
</p>

<h2>Who decides</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    The employee's <strong>manager</strong> (the person they report to, if linked to a login).
  </li>
  <li>Any workspace <strong>admin</strong>, for example for people without a manager.</li>
  <li>Never the person who asked: nobody approves their own leave.</li>
</ul>
<.callout>
  The right side of the Leave page shows who's away in the next 30 days, and employees on leave
  today get an <em>Away</em> badge in the directory.
</.callout>
